<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Brixen</h1><p class="st-meta">1027 CE – 1803 CE · capital Brixen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine prince-bishopric on the Brenner route, set up by emperors to guard the road to Italy, whose bishops ruled parts of Tyrol for nearly 800 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The bishopric moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saben-abbey">Säben</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> (Bressanone) around 990. In 1027 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Conrad II</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Hartwig the counties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inn-river">Inn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Eisack</a> valleys, and in 1091 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puster-valley">Puster valley</a> followed, so that loyal bishops would hold the passes on the imperial road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> entrusted their counties to advocates, chiefly the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, who gradually absorbed most of their power. The bishopric retained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruneck">Bruneck</a> and scattered lands. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-cusa">Nicholas of Cusa</a>, bishop from 1450, clashed bitterly with Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-archduke-of-austria">Sigismund of Tyrol</a> and was imprisoned in 1460.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The prince-bishopric was secularised in 1803 and annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1805 to 1814, and back to Austria. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and in 1964 the see moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>, becoming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-bolzano-brixen">Diocese of Bolzano-Brixen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-brixen.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Brixen" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: ZH2010 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_St_George%27s_Cross.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1027 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brixen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hartwig, Nicholas of Cusa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German, Ladin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy, Austria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bressanone, Hochstift Brixen, Bishopric of Bressanone</dd></dl></aside></div>
